                  <history>
                     <origin>
                        <origPlace>
                           <placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/INS0053SM">Sabayā Māryām</placeName>
                        </origPlace>
                        <origDate notBefore="1600" notAfter="1999">The manuscript consists of two codicological units. <ref target="#p1"/> is older and can be dated to the 17th century. If the note in
                           <ref target="#a2"/> refers to King <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS7102Minas">Minās</persName> (ʾAdmās Sagad) and was written during his reign, 
                           then the dating should be adjusted to the second half of the 16th century. <ref target="#p2"/> is of recent, 20th-century origin.</origDate>                     
                     </origin>
                     <provenance>It is unknown who was the original commissioner or owner of <ref target="#p1"/>.  The manuscript contains later ownership notes, written by secondary hands. 
                        At one point it was owned (and donated) by Priest <persName role="owner" ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15061GabMar">Gabra Māryām</persName> and his wife <persName role="owner" ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15062WalMar">Walatta Māryām</persName>
                        who appear in <ref target="#a4"/>. Also, <persName role="owner" ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15060WaldaAbEgz">Walda ʿĀbiya ʾƎgzi</persName> is
                        mentioned as the owner in <ref target="#a3"/> and, together with <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15063GabKidan">Gabra Kidān</persName>, <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15064WaldaSel">Walda Śǝllāse</persName>, <persName role="owner" ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15062WalMar">Walatta Māryām</persName>, 
                        <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15065TaklaGiyor">Takla Giyorgis</persName>, <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15066WalattaMuse">Walatta Muse</persName>, <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15067WalattaGior">Walatta Giyorgis</persName>, in a
                        prayer in <ref target="#a5"/>. <ref target="#p2"/> was most likely produced at the instigation of priest <persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS15068Barhe">Barḥe</persName>, s. <ref target="#a12"/>. 
                        It is attached to the centerfold of <ref target="#q20"/>, which belongs to <ref target="#p1"/>, by means of a twisted parchment strip.
                     </provenance>
                  </history>
               
